<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sir William Johnson, 1st Baronet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1715">1715</a>–<a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Sir William Johnson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir William Johnson was an Irish-born colonial official in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> who became British superintendent of Indian affairs for the northern colonies in 1756. Settled in the Mohawk valley, he learned the Mohawk language, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molly-brant">Molly Brant</a> as his consort and built close ties with the Iroquois. He led colonial and Iroquois forces to victory at Lake George in 1755, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-niagara">Fort Niagara</a> in 1759 and negotiated the Treaty of Fort Stanwix in 1768.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-johnson.jpg" alt="Sir William Johnson, 1st Baronet" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_William_Johnson_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
